From 442ba0be83aa4a36f6a8ed2fb92ae892de3c43a4
Subject: bevy: start the engine on web workers from one page call
Origin: https://github.com/robtfm/bevy/commit/442ba0be83aa4a36f6a8ed2fb92ae892de3c43a4
Date: 2026-09-23T11:42:35+01:00

`bevy::web_worker::start` takes the page's canvas and spawns the workers
the engine runs on: the render worker (the OffscreenCanvas, the wgpu
device and the render world), the compute workers (the ComputeTaskPool)
and the engine worker (the app world and winit's event loop). Every
worker runs the same script, shipped with bevy_internal as a wasm-bindgen
snippet, which instantiates the module over the page's shared memory and
calls bevy's own export for its role: the render, compute and attach
entries are bevy's, so the app supplies just its engine export, which
builds the app and runs it. An optional render-worker setup export runs
there before the render world exists, for patching WebGPU or providing
the wgpu resources.

Page functions, the JS hooks the engine looks up on `self`, are relayed
from every worker to the page's global. Their names come from the
declarations: the `#[page_functions]` attribute (bevy_derive) on a
`js_namespace = self` extern block re-emits the block and adds a generated
export listing its JS names, which the worker script finds by prefix and
installs relays for. No list to maintain and no link-section registry,
which linkme cannot provide on wasm32. Worker console output is mirrored
to the page, and a worker posts `__bevy_ready` once its entries return.
